The pattern: **before running an approach at full scale, identify the one assumption its cost advantage depends on, and test that assumption cheaply first.**

```js
// Cheap check: does the assumption hold on a small sample?
const sample = pages.slice(0, 5);
const sampleResults = await Promise.all(sample.map(fetchAndCheckStructure));
const structurePredictable = sampleResults.every(r => r.ingredientsFoundAt === 'div.ingredients');

if (structurePredictable) {
  // Cheap approach validated — targeted parse on all 500
  results = await Promise.all(pages.map(targetedParse));
} else {
  // Assumption failed — fall back to the more expensive but reliable approach
  results = await Promise.all(pages.map(fullPageScan));
}
```

The check itself has a real cost (5 fetches, in this case) — which means the pattern only pays off when the full-scale savings dwarf the check's cost. Checking 5 pages before processing 500 is a good trade. Checking 5 pages before processing 6 barely saves anything and adds a step for no real benefit — the same proportionality judgment lesson two of the previous module applied to planning first at all.

